{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Solanch Lopez, Business Community Manager - Economic Development presented the winners of the 2025 Holiday Window Contest, an annual initiative encouraging downtown merchants to decorate their storefronts during the holiday season to enhance the city\rquote s festive atmosphere. Four awards were announced: the Community Choice Award went to Ella Bella Rosario, which received 58% of community votes; Best Use of Lights was awarded to Joy Buy, a holiday pop-up at 332 Miracle Mile; Most Original was given to Intuition Stylist for its creative seasonal mannequin displays; and Best Overall was awarded to John Martin\rquote s Irish Pub, which transformed its entire restaurant into a Christmas pop-up bar with elaborate decorations. Each winner received a $300 prize. Both the Mayor and City Commissioners praised the creativity and effort of participating in businesses, noting that such initiatives foster community spirit and attract visitors to Coral Gables.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Belkys Perez, Director-Economic Development Department announced that Coral Gables achieved Gold Level Recognition in the Communities of Excellence program, making it the first community eligible to apply for the National Malcolm Baldrige Quality Award in 2026. This recognition reflects years of collaborative efforts since 2019 to improve health, safety, education, and quality of life. She presented data showing a significant reduction in violent crime rates compared to Miami, Florida, and national averages, as well as Coral Gables High School graduation rates improving dramatically to reach the top 20th percentile nationwide. The presentation outlined the rigorous evaluation process, which requires trend data and comparative analysis, and discussed next steps for applying for the national award. The Mayor and City Commissioners commended Ms. Perez, Baptist Health, the Chamber of Commerce, and other partners for their dedication to maintaining Coral Gables\rquote reputation for excellence.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission recognized Giralda Cleaners, located at 123 Giralda Avenue, as a recipient of the 2025 Commercial Beautification Award. Celebrating its 30th anniversary this month, the business was honored for maintaining and enhancing its storefront landscaping, including improvements coordinated with city-led streetscape projects. The Mayor and City Commissioners praised the owner\rquote s commitment to beautification and noted that such efforts contribute to Coral Gables\rquote distinctive charm and appeal, reinforcing the city\rquote s reputation for elegance and attention to detail.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission proclaimed January 13, 2026, as \ldblquote Ana Alicia Fernandez Day\rdblquote in Coral Gables, honoring her and her mother, Alicia Fernandez, for their resilience and advocacy following a tragic boating accident in 1995. The proclamation highlighted Alicia\rquote s tireless care for her daughter and her efforts to promote boating safety. Officer Randy Hoff, credited with saving Ana Alicia\rquote s life, shared his experience, noting that the incident catalyzed the city\rquote s boating safety programs. The Mayor and City Commissioners and public safety officials praised Alicia\rquote s unwavering dedication, calling her story a testament to love, perseverance, and community support. Emotional remarks from the officials emphasized the importance of recognizing caregivers and first responders who go above and beyond, and the presentation concluded with heartfelt gratitude and a commemorative photo.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Driver Engineer John Jensen was honored by Fire Chief de la Rosa for his exceptional service. The Chief recognized him for his commitment to equipment readiness and safety, as well as his leadership and dedication to public safety. \line The Mayor and City Commissioners spoke of the crucial role this position plays in emergency responses and the city's overall preparedness.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sl276\slmult1\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Fire Chief de la Rosa honored Driver Engineer Brandon Ogden for his exceptional service and praised him for his expertise in navigating Coral Gables' complex streets and maintaining the Fire Department's apparatus standards, as well as his leadership and dedication to public safety. The Mayor and City Commissioners spoke on the crucial role these positions play in emergency response and the city's overall preparedness.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission recognized Nicole Gautney, Part-Time Sustainability Coordinator, for her outstanding contributions to environmental initiatives and community engagement through the \ldblquote Keep Coral Gables Beautiful Program\rdblquote . Nicole\rquote s efforts include organizing over 300 events, drafting grant applications, and promoting sustainability citywide. Commissioners lauded her enthusiasm, leadership, and commitment to making Coral Gables a greener, more sustainable community, noting her role as an ambassador for environmental stewardship.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Asst. Police Chief Hanlon made the presentation honoring Mercedes Paulino, Neighborhood Safety Aide (NSA), as "Employee of the Month" for November 2025 because of her proactive approach to community safety and exceptional customer service. The Mayor and City Commissioners and Asst. Police Chief Hanlon highlighted the crucial role NSAs play in reducing crime and building relationships with residents and noted their efforts during the holiday season to prevent package thefts. The discussion underscored the importance of layered security measures and community engagement in maintaining Coral Gables' reputation as one of the safest cities in the region. The Mayor and City Commissioners expressed gratitude for the dedication of NSAs and their contributions to public safety.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

