{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The City Commission proclaimed February 10, 2026, as Snows Jewelers Day, honoring the family-owned business for over five decades of service on Miracle Mile. The proclamation highlighted Judy Snow\rquote s groundbreaking achievement as Florida\rquote s first female graduate gemologist in 1978 and the store\rquote s reputation for exceptional craftsmanship and customer service. Eddie Snow reflected on the business\rquote s resilience through economic downturns, recessions, and even an armed robbery, sharing anecdotes that illustrated the store\rquote s deep roots in Coral Gables. He announced plans to transition to a concierge jewelry service after the storefront closes in April 2026, ensuring continuity of service for loyal customers. Mayor Lago and commissioners praised the Snow family\rquote s contributions to the city\rquote s economic vitality and cultural identity, pledging to promote their new venture through city communications. Commissioners also recognized Judy Snow as a trailblazer for women in business and celebrated the family\rquote s multigenerational ties to Coral Gables.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The City Commission proclaimed February 2026 as Black History Month in Coral Gables, honoring the historic contributions of Black residents to the city\rquote s development. The proclamation acknowledged the McFarland and Golden Gate neighborhoods, originally settled by Bahamian and Southern Black families who provided essential labor for George Merrick\rquote s vision of Coral Gables. Community representatives delivered a comprehensive historical narrative, citing milestones such as the founding of St. Mary\rquote s First Missionary Baptist Church in 1924, the creation of the Coral Gables Improvement Association in the 1940s, and the establishment of scholarships through the Lola B. Walker Homeowners Association. Mayor Lago announced plans for a sculpture honoring these communities to be installed prominently in front of City Hall in September 2026, symbolizing their central role in the city\rquote s history. Residents shared personal stories of multigenerational ties to Coral Gables, expressing pride in their heritage and gratitude for the city\rquote s efforts to preserve it. Commissioners reaffirmed their commitment to inclusivity and historical preservation, emphasizing that Coral Gables is one unified community.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The City of Coral Gables formally committed to participating in Keep America Beautiful\rquote s Greatest American Cleanup as part of America\rquote s 250th anniversary celebrations. The declaration outlined plans for at least three community cleanups, two recycling or beautification events, and one celebration event between March and July 2026. Staff detailed upcoming initiatives, including a sustainability village at the farmers market, invasive species removal at Pinewood Cemetery, Earth Month activities, and a recycling drive-through on April 25. Vice Mayor Anderson announced the launch of a composting program at the Coral Gables Library and highlighted other environmental projects such as living seawalls and a micro-forest to improve air quality and reduce noise pollution. Mayor Lago pledged a $500 prize for the individual collecting the most litter during cleanup events to encourage participation. The discussion underscored Coral Gables\rquote leadership in sustainability and its commitment to civic engagement in environmental stewardship.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Plaza Coral Gables received the 2025 Commercial Beautification Award for its exceptional landscaping, maintenance, and environmental leadership. The development achieved LEED certification across nine projects, setting a benchmark for sustainable construction in Coral Gables. Jose Antonio Perez accepted the award, emphasizing the project\rquote s role as a legacy for the city and its commitment to hosting community events and supporting philanthropic initiatives. Mayor Lago praised the Plaza for its meticulous upkeep, noting its impact on property values and quality of life. Jorge L. Arrizurieta, President and Chief Exec. Officer/Coral Gables Chamber of Commerce commended The Plaza for expanding the downtown footprint and creating a vibrant mixed-use environment that attracts residents and visitors. The award presentation highlighted the Plaza\rquote s role as a model for future development and its contributions to Coral Gables\rquote reputation as a premier destination for business and leisure.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Division Chief Louis Fajardo was honored as Firefighter of the Month for his humanitarian efforts beyond city borders. After learning of a tragic accident in the Bahamas where victims could not be extricated due to lack of equipment, he coordinated donations of life-saving tools similar to the Jaws of Life and ensured their delivery to local first responders. His initiative reflects Coral Gables Fire Department\rquote s ethos of service and global responsibility. In conclusion he expressed gratitude to his colleagues and vendors who supported the effort, emphasizing his vision to extend training opportunities to neighboring cities. Mayor Lago commended him on his leadership and selflessness, noting that such acts strengthen regional safety and exemplify the city\rquote s values.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Emergency Dispatch Operator Monica Dinescu was recognized as Operator of the Year for 2025 for her professionalism and empathy during high-stress calls. One notable incident involved assisting a caller in severe emotional distress where Dinescu maintained composure, provided reassurance, and coordinated resources effectively. Her peers describe her as a standard-bearer of excellence, known for proactive communication and thorough research. Upon accepting the award, she thanked her supervisors and team for their support and pride in serving Coral Gables for nine years. The award presentation highlighted the critical role of dispatch operators in public safety and their ability to manage crises with compassion and precision.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Officer Falcao received the Officer of the Year Award for 2025 for his investigative work in solving a complex hit-and-run case that left two residents seriously injured. His efforts included leveraging advanced technology such as ALPR systems, securing cell site warrants, and coordinating with multiple jurisdictions, ultimately leading to the suspect\rquote s arrest and recovery of the vehicle. The investigation also resulted in the seizure of narcotics, removing additional threats from the community. He credited teamwork and expressed humility, noting that the case\rquote s success was a collective effort. Mayor Lago praised his dedication, emphasizing the importance of persistence and attention to detail in law enforcement and reaffirmed Coral Gables\rquote commitment to maintaining one of the safest cities in the nation through proactive policing and technological innovation.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

