{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sl276\slmult1\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Vice Mayor Rhonda Anderson read a detailed proclamation honoring Women\rquote s History Month and recognizing the contributions of women to American democracy, public service, and civic leadership. Special acknowledgment was given to Miami-Dade County Supervisor of Elections Alina Garc\'eda, the county\rquote s first elected person in that role and a champion of election integrity and accessibility. Garc\'eda delivered heartfelt remarks about her Cuban upbringing, deep commitment to democracy, and the responsibility she feels to protect the electoral process for future generations. Commissioners praised her responsiveness, leadership, and dedication during Coral Gables\rquote upcoming mail-ballot referendum. Mayor Lago emphasized her exceptional outreach across Miami-Dade and her unwavering defense of democratic processes.\par
\pard\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Former Mayor Don Slesnick, serving as Chair of the Florida History Committee, presented official State of Florida certificates recognizing both the Granada Golf Course and the Biltmore (Bill Moore) Golf Course as historic members of the Florida Historic Golf Trail. He spoke of the Trail\rquote s growing tourism potential and highlighted that Coral Gables is fortunate to have two of Florida\rquote s oldest and most iconic courses included. Representatives from the Biltmore, including President Tom Prescott and Golf Director Lara Pavlov, accepted the plaques with gratitude. He also recounted the Biltmore course\rquote s historic 1926 opening exhibition featuring golf legends Bobby Jones, Tommy Armour, Gene Sarazen, and Leo Diegel and recognized Granada as Florida\rquote s first grass-green nine-hole course, opened in 1923 with $1 green fees. Commissioners praised the historic importance of both courses and the dedicated stewardship by the City and its partners. \cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Architect Anna Alice was honored for her extensive architectural documentation project, \i Inking History\i0 , a multi-year effort producing meticulously scaled ink drawings of Coral Gables\rquote historic buildings. Her work has now earned national recognition: the Institute of Classical Architecture and Art awarded her the Medal for Research and Documentation under the prestigious Addison Mizner Awards. Former Mayor Slesnick explained that the project has become known nationally as the \ldblquote Civic Face of Coral Gables.\rdblquote Anna announced that limited-edition prints are sold through the Coral Gables Community Foundation to raise funds for local preservation. Commissioners shared personal stories of purchasing and displaying the pieces, praising the artistic quality and preservation value of her work. A drawing of the historic Water Tower was presented to adorn a blank wall at Birdie Bistro as a gift to the City. \cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission declared \ldblquote Suddenly Sleepy Saturday,\rdblquote raising awareness of narcolepsy and related sleep disorders. Resident Cassandra Murphy spoke emotionally about her diagnosis of narcolepsy type 1, her struggles with excessive daytime sleepiness, a serious car accident near her home, and leaving her legal career of 15 years due to safety concerns. She expressed gratitude to the City for elevating awareness during Sleep Awareness Week, noting her acceptance into an advanced clinical trial at Stanford with the nation\rquote s top narcolepsy specialist. Commissioners thanked her for her courage and advocacy, and the Mayor Lago directed staff to publish educational resources and her story in the City\rquote s next newsletter. \cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The City declared April as Autism Acceptance Month and welcomed back Raquel Elejabarrieta, former ADA Coordinator and longtime leader of the City\rquote s Disability Affairs Board. Staff praised her decade of transformative work, including establishing the annual Autism Acceptance Flag (cocreated with Crystal Academy students), launching Adventure Day, expanding Best Buddies and Project Victory partnerships, and securing new self-contained autism classrooms at Ponce Middle School and Coral Gables Senior High. Board members highlighted Raquel\rquote s pivotal role in creating the G.A.I.D. (Guide) Program, which allows families to register vulnerable residents with the Police Department for emergency support. Commissioners shared personal reflections on the impact of her work and emphasized continued commitment to inclusion. During the ceremony Fire Chief de la Rosa awarded Raquel a symbolic white fire helmet, representing the highest honor and leadership. Ms. Elejabarrieta humbly and graciously thanked the City Commission and Fire Chief for the recognition and expressed how much she loved her work with the City and was especially proud of the strides made in autism acceptance."\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Matthew Anderson, Asst. Director- Sustainability and Parking Department presented the Earth Month proclamation, outlining Coral Gables\rquote leadership in environmental protection and its extensive schedule of more than 11 public events planned in April. Highlights included the City\rquote s long-running recycling drive-through events (diverting over 1 million pounds of waste), the sustainability village at the Farmers Market, Biscayne Bay restoration initiatives, the launch of a citywide composting program on April 20, and new efforts to expand battery and plastic-film recycling. Staff emphasized that Coral Gables\rquote environment is central to its economic vitality, highlighting the City\rquote s tree canopy, waterway health, and climate preparedness. Commissioners praised the innovation and partnership among City departments and community organizations. \cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 He stated that \ldblquote Keep Coral Gables Beautiful\rdblquote received the 2025 National Innovation Award from Keep America Beautiful. The award honors groundbreaking GIS mapping tools developed by an intern Thiago Esslinger in collaboration with the Sustainability and IT Departments. These tools increase transparency by displaying litter index data and cleanup event impacts. Thiago described the internship as transformative and meaningful, noting that his work had real-world impact beyond the classroom. Commissioners celebrated the City\rquote s national recognition and the increasing sophistication of its environmental data initiatives. \cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Police Chief Hudak announced that the Coral Gables Police Department has achieved its 10th national accreditation from the Commission on Accreditation for Law Enforcement Agencies (CALEA), placing the department among the top 1% of agencies nationwide. Accreditation Manager Alex Rafi explained the rigorous multi-year review process, including hundreds of policy checks, ride-alongs, interviews, and facility inspections. The chief reflected on the department\rquote s evolution since its first accreditation decades ago and praised the professionalism, culture of excellence, and commitment to continuous improvement embraced by current officers and staff. Commissioners congratulated the department for its longstanding national reputation. \cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
