\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission received a presentation from the Landscape Beautification Advisory Board recognizing outstanding residential properties throughout Coral Gables for their landscaping and beautification efforts. Staff explained that the board spends the year canvassing neighborhoods across seven designated zones in the city, identifying properties that best exemplify sustainable landscaping, maintenance, and visual enhancement of the community. Award recipients were selected through a nomination and voting process conducted by the board members. During the presentation, several homeowners and representatives were recognized and presented with plaques. One recipient emphasized that the true credit belonged to the landscaping personnel responsible for maintaining the property daily, specifically recognizing longtime groundskeeper Rafael Garcia for his dedication and hard work. Commissioners thanked all recipients for contributing to the beauty and quality of life of Coral Gables, emphasizing that the city\rquote s identity as \ldblquote The City Beautiful\rdblquote depends greatly on the pride residents take in maintaining their properties.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission proclaimed April 14, 2026, as Carlos Migoya Day in Coral Gables in recognition of his nearly fifteen years of service as President and CEO of Jackson Health System and his decades of leadership in banking, government, and public service. The proclamation detailed his accomplishments in stabilizing and transforming Jackson Health System from a financially distressed institution into one of the nation\rquote s leading public healthcare systems. His leadership included overseeing a $2 billion capital improvement initiative funded through a voter-approved bond program, modernizing healthcare infrastructure throughout Miami-Dade County. Mayor Lago delivered extensive remarks reflecting on Migoya\rquote s mentorship, leadership, and willingness to provide guidance both professionally and personally. Former Miami Mayor Francis Suarez and community leader Stuart Miller also spoke, highlighting Migoya\rquote s courage during difficult fiscal periods, his commitment to public service, and his role in strengthening relationships between Jackson Health System and the University of Miami. Migoya reflected on his immigrant experience, his appreciation for Coral Gables, and his desire to give back to the community that helped shape his life. The mayor concluded the presentation by presenting Migoya with the Key to the City of Coral Gables.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission proclaimed April 15, 2026, as American Airlines Day in Coral Gables in celebration of the airline\rquote s 100th anniversary. The proclamation recognized American Airlines\rquote transformative impact on South Florida through the establishment and continued growth of its Miami hub, which now serves as a major gateway connecting the United States with Latin America, the Caribbean, Europe, and other international destinations. Representatives from American Airlines emphasized the company\rquote s deep relationship with Coral Gables, noting that many employees reside in the city and contribute to its civic life. Speakers highlighted the airline\rquote s role in strengthening tourism, trade, and international business in the region. Mayor Lago particularly recognized longtime Coral Gables resident and American Airlines employee Zully Pardo for her dedication both to the airline and the community, describing her as an outstanding ambassador for both organizations.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Aptos;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission proclaimed April 17, 2026, as Veterans of the Bay of Pigs Day in Coral Gables in honor of Brigade 2506 and the 65th anniversary of the Bay of Pigs invasion. The proclamation recognized the courage and sacrifice of the Cuban exiles who participated in the 1961 mission to liberate Cuba from communism, acknowledging the hardships endured by those who were imprisoned following the invasion. Rafael Montalvo, president of the Brigade 2506 Veterans Association, addressed the Commission and reflected on his experience as an 18-year-old member of the brigade, emphasizing the continuing importance of preserving the lessons of freedom and democracy. Discussion also focused on the upcoming opening of the new Bay of Pigs Museum in Little Havana, which will preserve the brigade\rquote s history and educate future generations. Mayor Lago spoke personally about his family\rquote s connection to the Cuban exile community and praised the veterans for their patriotism, sacrifice, and lifelong commitment to freedom.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission proclaimed April 24, 2026, as Arbor Day in Coral Gables, celebrating the city\rquote s longstanding commitment to urban forestry and environmental stewardship. The proclamation highlighted the historical significance of Arbor Day and recognized Coral Gables\rquote 41st consecutive year as a Tree City USA community. Commissioners and staff emphasized the importance of the city\rquote s tree canopy in enhancing quality of life, improving environmental conditions, and maintaining the city\rquote s aesthetic character. The presentation included recognition of the Landscape Beautification Advisory Board and city staff responsible for maintaining public landscaping and urban forestry efforts. Officials praised Public Works employees for their day-to-day dedication to maintaining streetscapes, parks, flowers, and trees throughout the city, noting that their efforts often go unnoticed despite being critical to preserving the city\rquote s beauty.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission proclaimed April 2026 as Parkinson\rquote s Awareness Month in Coral Gables. The proclamation outlined the increasing prevalence of Parkinson\rquote s disease and the significant physical, emotional, and financial impacts it has on patients and families. A representative from the Parkinson\rquote s Foundation discussed the organization\rquote s mission of improving care and advancing research toward a cure, while also highlighting the foundation\rquote s support services and multilingual helpline for patients and caregivers. Commissioners shared personal experiences with the disease, including the impact Parkinson\rquote s had on family members, emphasizing the importance of awareness, research, and community support for those affected.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission proclaimed April 2026 as Landscape Architecture Month in Coral Gables. The proclamation recognized the profession of landscape architecture for its role in shaping healthy, sustainable, resilient, and aesthetically pleasing public and private spaces. Assistant Public Works Director Deena Bell and representatives from the American Society of Landscape Architects spoke about the importance of thoughtful urban design, environmental stewardship, and maintaining high standards in public spaces. The presentation emphasized that Coral Gables itself is a strong example of landscape architecture principles in practice, particularly through its tree-lined streets, public spaces, and commitment to preserving its historical character.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission proclaimed June 21-27, 2026, as Ocean Pollution Awareness Week in Coral Gables. The presentation recognized the efforts of Ocean Precious, a nonprofit organization founded by University of Miami student Vaishnavi Ratnam, focused on reducing ocean pollution and promoting environmental sustainability. Ratnam described her organization\rquote s accomplishments in recycling plastic waste and educating students about environmental stewardship. Commissioners emphasized the economic and environmental importance of protecting South Florida\rquote s waterways and coastlines, noting that clean oceans and healthy marine ecosystems are essential not only to tourism and property values but also to the region\rquote s overall quality of life.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Lieutenant Christian Garcia was recognized as Firefighter of the Month for March 2026 for his leadership and dedication within the Coral Gables Fire Department\rquote s Technical Rescue Team. Fire Department leadership described Garcia as a highly respected mentor and instructor who consistently trains and motivates firefighters to maintain the highest professional standards. Garcia spoke about the importance of teamwork, continuous training, and maintaining a commitment to excellence in technical rescue operations. He emphasized that success in the fire service depends on discipline, preparation, and dedication to serving the community at the highest level.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Driver Engineer Ryan Verdesia was recognized as Firefighter of the Month for April 2026 following his completion of Broward Sheriff\rquote s Office SWAT paramedic training, where he graduated as the top performer and class leader. Verdesia reflected on the demanding physical and mental challenges of the program and emphasized the importance of maintaining high standards in emergency response operations. He also expressed gratitude to his family, particularly his wife, for supporting him throughout the rigorous training process. Fire Department leadership praised his commitment and noted that his accomplishments reflect the department\rquote s broader culture of professionalism and excellence.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 Officer Alexandra Vega was recognized as Officer of the Month for February 2026 for her proactive police work during her first weeks as a patrol officer. Police leadership explained that Vega successfully identified and helped apprehend a repeat offender involved in multiple theft incidents within Coral Gables. Even while off duty, Vega recognized the suspect while driving to work and coordinated with on-duty personnel and detectives to facilitate the arrest. The department praised her diligence, initiative, and strong instincts early in her law enforcement career. Vega thanked her supervisors, training officers, colleagues, family, and friends for their support and stated that the recognition reflected the teamwork and professionalism of the entire department.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\kerning2\f0\fs24 The Commission recognized Charles Carr, the city\rquote s urban forester, as Employee of the Month. Carr was praised for his extensive responsibilities managing the city\rquote s tree canopy, overseeing tree maintenance operations, reviewing pruning and removal permits, coordinating emergency responses to fallen branches, and supervising contracts involving more than 30,000 city trees. Staff highlighted Carr\rquote s dedication to environmental stewardship and resident service, including a story in which he personally retrieved a balloon from a tree to prevent environmental harm and avoid unnecessary city expenses. Commissioners acknowledged the complexity of his work, particularly the challenges associated with balancing resident concerns, environmental preservation, and public safety while maintaining the city\rquote s iconic tree canopy.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
}

