{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago presented Certificates of Recognition to three professional permit expeditors - Dayron Garcia, Lucy Lopez and Mannix Fernandez - who volunteered their time and expertise to provide candid, in-depth feedback on the city's permitting process as part of an ongoing effort to fully revamp the department. Each submitted detailed PowerPoint presentations totaling approximately 30 pages of insight, guidance, and recommendations. Mayor Lago emphasized that he sought honest assessments - what works, what can be improved, and what is broken - from people who use the system daily at a professional level. Mannix was unable to attend due to his wife's cancer treatment but still participated fully. The Mayor shared the origin story of the city's in-home permitting program, which now processes over 300 permits per year for elderly, disabled, and veteran residents - an idea first brought to him by Dayron during \ldblquote Mayor\rquote s Office Hours. Both present recipients expressed deep appreciation for the recognition and their personal investment in the city's improvement. Lucy also took the opportunity to wish her mother Happy Birthday and for instilling the importance of hard work.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 The City Commission proclaimed May 3-9, 2026 as National Travel and Tourism Week in Coral Gables. The proclamation highlights tourism as an economic powerhouse generating $133 billion in economic impact statewide and supporting over 1.8 million Florida jobs in 2025. Miami-Dade County welcomed more than 28 million visitors contributing approximately $21 billion in spending and supporting over 200,000 local jobs. GMCVB Chief Operating Officer Rolando Aedo accompanied by his colleague GMCVB Director-Corporate Communications & External Affairs Richard Gibbs, highlighted Coral Gables' tourism assets including 12+ hotels, 2,000+ rooms, the Venetian Pool reopening, the Biltmore Hotel centennial, a vibrant restaurant scene participating in Miami Spice and the Michelin program, and the city's Coral Gables Museum visitor center. He noted the city's role as home to FIFA and the FIFA World Cup 2026 host committee and recognized the Hyatt Coral Gables as the highest-achieving sustainable hotel in Miami-Dade County. The proclamation was read into the record by Commissioner Lara.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 The City Commission proclaimed May 5, 2026 - Cinco de Mayo - as Cielo Tejido Day in Coral Gables. Cielo Tejido ("Woven Sky") is a large-scale public art installation on Giralda Plaza created by approximately 200 women artisans from a collective work in Jalisco, Mexico, using a crochet craft tradition passed down through generations. The installation drew 5,300 attendees to the Giralda Live launch event - well above the typical 3,400-3,600 - energizing downtown businesses and foot traffic. The installation runs for three months followed by three months of a complementary umbrella display. City staff Boris Lazo, Hamlet Garcia, and Leticia Perez worked through the night to meet the installation deadline in partnership with the Consulate of Mexico's cultural team. The Consul General spoke in both Spanish and English, calling the date deeply symbolic and praising the project as a bridge between peoples and cultures. Mayor Lago and the City Commission thanked Director Perez for her efforts. Commissioner Fernandez also credited Commissioner Castro for keeping the initiative on the agenda for two years. Commissioner Lara then read the proclamation.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 The City Commission proclaimed May 6, 2026 as Villagers Day in Coral Gables, honoring the Miami Villagers Inc. on their 60th anniversary. Founded in 1966, the Villagers are the oldest historic preservation organization in Miami-Dade County, operating entirely through 180+ volunteers. They were born out of the effort to save Coral Gables' iconic Douglas Entrance from demolition for a grocery store. Over six decades they have awarded more than 275 grants to over 90 local endangered sites, funded over 200 scholarships to Florida University students in architecture and historic preservation, established the Dade Heritage Trust in 1972, and released emergency funds following Hurricane Irma to stabilize historic sites. This year alone the organization awarded over $160,000 in grants to organizations including the Merrick House, Plymouth Congregational Church, Coral Gables Women's Club, Coral Gables Prep Academy, and Sanctuary for the Arts. The proclamation was read into the record by Vice Mayor Anderson.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 The City Commission proclaimed May 2026 as National Historic Preservation Month, aligned with the 2026 national theme "Harnessing the Power of Place" and the nation's 250th anniversary. This item encompassed three related proclamations and presentations: (1) The main Historic Preservation Month proclamation recognizing the city's preservation program, which annually produces 14 new Register listings, 100 significance determination letters, 185 Certificates of Appropriateness, and 300 inspections. Board Chair Kelly Shield gave an in-depth presentation on the economic and cultural value of preservation, the city's 13 historic districts and 7 villages, TDR programs, and a proposed apartment building acquisition opportunity. (2) Chinese Village Day (May 3, 2026) - marking the centennial of Henry Killam Murphy's 1926 Beijing-inspired village on Riviera Drive, the only such enclave in the continental United States, celebrated with a Yale scholar lecture and a public tour. (3) Pinewood Cemetery - Board Chair John Swain presented the ongoing restoration of the 1896 pioneer cemetery near Coco Plum Circle. Six volunteer cleanup events have removed over 22,000 pounds of invasive plants, and a formal restoration plan with timeline and budget is being finalized. The cemetery holds approximately 200 burials, many unknown, and is threatened by 70 large Australian pines requiring careful, phased removal to avoid disturbing gravesites. Vice Mayor Anderson read the proclamation.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 The City Commission proclaimed April 12-18, 2026 as National Public Safety Telecommunications Week in Coral Gables, honoring the 911 communications operators and dispatchers who serve as the city's first line of public safety. Police Chief Hudak acknowledged Communications Manager Diaz and Major David Lemon for their efforts in putting the department on track to becoming triple rated in the accreditation process. He also emphasized that unlike field officers who generally know what they are responding to, dispatchers answer each call with no advance knowledge - whether it\rquote s a medical emergency, crime in progress, or a personal crisis - and must respond with consistent professionalism every time. The proclamation recognizes Communications Operators of the Year nominated by department personnel and honored during the week. The item was read alongside the National Police Week proclamation. Commissioner Fernandez read the proclamation.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 The City Commission proclaimed May 11-17, 2026 as National Police Week in Coral Gables, honoring law enforcement officers killed in the line of duty. The proclamation notes more than 800,000 officers serving nationwide, and since the first recorded line-of-duty death in 1786, over 24,000 officers have made the ultimate sacrifice - including 10 members of the Coral Gables Police Department whose names are engraved on the National Law Enforcement Officers Memorial in Washington, D.C. May15 is designated Peace Officers Memorial Day. The proclamation was delivered with particular weight given that a Coral Gables officer was murdered in the line of duty in November 2025. A memorial ceremony was scheduled for Thursday at 3:00 p.m. at the Police Department to honor fallen members and the officers who continue to serve. The Mayor and Commissioners all spoke of their respect for the City\rquote s Police Department. Mayor Lago read the proclamation.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 Driver Engineer Christina Bola\'f1os was recognized as Firefighter of the Month for May 2026. In addition to her primary role operating fire trucks, Bola\'f1os took on numerous additional projects including documentation for the department's accreditation process, enhancing logistics and procurement procedures, and spearheading the department's first-ever EMS accreditation effort, expected to be completed in summer 2026. Fire Chief de la Rosa noted her exceptional sense of ownership over projects that benefit the entire department. Bola\'f1os thanked the department and, echoing the chief's philosophy of "own it," described the EMS accreditation as her opportunity to take full ownership of an important initiative.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 Officer Alexander Rodriguez was presented with the 2025 Robert de Kort Memorial Award - the Coral Gables Fraternal Order of Police Lodge #7's highest honor, named after Officer Robert de Kort who was shot in the line of duty on January 21, 1972, and gave his life attempting to drive himself to the hospital after apprehending two armed suspects at a liquor store alarm call. FOP President explained the award recognizes the totality of an officer's courage, character, and commitment. Officer Rodriguez was selected over two other distinguished nominees: Officer Chris Challenger, recognized for promoting brotherhood within the FOP, and Officer Robbie Thompson, who played a vital role in dismantling a human trafficking ring in January 2025. Rodriguez's notable 2025 incidents included locating and apprehending a stolen vehicle suspect through proactive surveillance outside city jurisdiction; coordinating a high-speed wrong-way pursuit on US-1; and - most notably - connecting with and talking down a suicidal juvenile standing on a parking garage ledge, earning a separate lifesaving award later that year. Rodriguez credited his colleagues, saying every incident reflected teamwork, and pledged to continue delivering the high level of service Coral Gables is known for.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\sa480\qj\f0\fs24 The IT Department presented the achievements of the city's Smart City Research Laboratory, receiving recognition for three major awards: the international Smart 20 Award (presented in Raleigh, NC) for its workforce development program; the GeoGov Innovation in Education, Workforce Development and Capacity Building Award from the world's largest geospatial organization; and the Tech Talent Pipeline Workforce Development Award from Miami Tech Works. The lab hosted over 200 interns across 10 years from high schools, undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral programs at UM, FIU, and Miami Dade College, providing real-world challenges, micro-credentials, and career placement connections. Featured projects included a sidewalk speed violation sensor prototype, AI voice agents, NFC digital business cards, 3D printing for fabrication, drone-based building information models, augmented reality cultural experiences, and a digital twin program. Mark Ebert introduced current interns and lab staff, noting two new cohorts - including five FIU Breakthrough Sprinternship students and two NIST RAMPS program participants - had started the previous day. Mayor Lago expressed strong support for proposed future lab expansion following City Hall\rquote s renovation and praised the department's national reputation and generosity toward peer cities.\cf1\f1\par
}

