{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago took a personal point of privilege to pay tribute to Pedro V. Roig, a long-time Coral Gables resident and close friend and mentor to Mayor Lago and his family, who passed away recently. Mayor Lago described Roig as a quiet, dignified man of great intellectual depth who frequently welcomed him into his home for hours-long conversations about art, Cuban history, politics, and faith. The Mayor read a formal Resolution of Condolence into the record, which recognized Roig as an attorney, historian, educator, author, and advocate for human rights. The resolution noted that Roig was a graduate of the University of Miami and St. Thomas University School of Law, a veteran of Assault Brigade 2506 (the Cuban exile force that fought in the Bay of Pigs invasion), and that he was appointed by President George H.W. Bush to the National Advisory Board of Education and by President George W. Bush as Director of the Office of Cuban Broadcasting (Radio and Television Marti). Roig was also affiliated with the University of Miami's Institute of Cuban and Cuban American Studies, and served as counsel at the law firm Herzl, Dreyfus & Dempsey, based at the historic Biltmore Hotel. Members of the Roig family were present and offered remarks, including his niece Nikki Whiting, a Coral Gables resident, who described Roig as a father figure to her mother during the 20 years her grandfather was a political prisoner in Cuba. Richard Kuper, another family member stated that Roig, until his final moments, remained devoted to the cause of freedom and the liberation of Cuba. Mayor Lago presented the Roig family with a posthumous Key to the City, noting it was the first time he had extended the honor to someone who had passed away.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago took a personal point of privilege to honor his administrative assistant, Nicole Guevara, who is departing City Hall to pursue a Juris Doctor degree at Loyola University Chicago School of Law. He praised her professionalism, kindness, and positive impact on the mayor's office since joining in July 2025, and read a proclamation officially declaring May 19, 2026 as "Nicole Guevara Day" in Coral Gables in recognition of her service and achievements. Ms. Guevara thanked the mayor and staff, noting the experience had been an honor and had inspired her to consider a future career in public service, and expressed appreciation to Laura for her role in preparing the proclamation. The commission then paused for a group photo to commemorate the occasion.\fs22\tab\par
\pard\cf1\f1\fs24\par
}

