}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Chairwoman Rojas, representing District Six of Miami-Dade County Public Schools (which encompasses the Coral Gables feeder pattern), delivered remarks celebrating educators and the teaching profession. She spoke about the district's accomplishments, including an A-rated designation for six consecutive years and a graduation rate of 95.4%, calling these achievements a reflection of the collective effort of teachers, administrators, and community partners. She thanked the City's School and Community Relations Board and recognized all participating principals in attendance. The following Teachers of the Year and Rookie Teachers of the Year from Coral Gables-area schools were recognized. Mayor Lago, Commissioner Fernandez, Vice Mayor Anderson, and Commissioner Lara all offered remarks praising the teaching profession and recognizing the City's strong and improving school system. Mayor Lago proposed future recognition of teachers dedicated to students with disabilities. Vice Mayor Anderson suggested improvements to how the city communicates public school magnet programs to residents and real estate professionals. Commissioner Lara spoke with pride as the only Coral Gables Senior High School graduate on the dais, noting major recent investments in the school including a new football field and an upcoming gymnasium. Mr. Sam Joseph, representing Literacy Fest, offered remarks via Zoom, congratulating the honorees and praising the city for annually recognizing educators.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Belkys Perez-Director of the City's Economic Development Department presented this item, recognizing the City's partnership with the Consulate General of Mexico. The Sip and Shop! event, held along Miracle Mile and Giralda Plaza, was organized in collaboration with the Mexican Consulate, which donated all the spirits (mezcal, tequila, and wine) for the ticketed event. Participating in retailers hosted tastings, drawing attendees into shops they might not have otherwise visited. Each edition of Sip and Shop! includes a charitable component; the Mexican Consulate designated the Marsoni Foundation as the beneficiary organization. The funds raised will support a Mexican national, Jacobo, who has been hospitalized in Miami for approximately one year awaiting a multi-organ transplant following a violent incident. Consul General Rutilio Escandon and Marsoni Foundation, Inc President Dr. Ruth Martinez were present to accept the donation check. Mayor Lago highlighted the Consulate's broader contributions to the city, including organizing the annual Dia de los Muertos event that attracts thousands of visitors and the \ldblquote Cielo Tejido\rdblquote (A Woven Sky) cultural installation along Miracle Mile and Giralda Plaza.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Commissioner Lara read the proclamation declaring May 17-23, 2026 as Emergency Medical Services Week in Coral Gables. The proclamation recognized the critical and often life-saving work performed by paramedics, EMTs, firefighters, emergency nurses, emergency physicians, educators, and administrators who constitute the City's EMS system, including their role in disaster response, injury prevention, and homeland security. Chief De La Rosa highlighted this year's national EMS theme - "Improving Outcomes Together" - and described the coordinated system in place: 911 communications personnel providing pre-arrival caller guidance, firefighter paramedics delivering advanced care with ongoing training and cutting-edge equipment, and coordination with hospital partners that allows stroke patients to bypass standard triage and go directly to the cath lab. He noted that CPR was being performed by bystanders prior to firefighter arrival in 84% of cardiac arrest cases, a result of community CPR education and police dispatch with AEDs.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Assistant Chief Jones presented the EMS Crew of the Year Award in keeping with the theme of "Improving Outcomes Together." The honorees were:\par
\bullet Lieutenant Samantha Putz Fernandez\par
\bullet Firefighter/Paramedic J.P. Milan\par
\bullet Firefighter/Paramedic Joseph Lovins\par
All three serve on Rescue 5. The crew was recognized for their exceptional compassion and professionalism in every patient interaction, and for the seamless, nearly wordless coordination that reflects deep teamwork and mutual understanding. Lieutenant Fernandez delivered brief remarks on behalf of the crew, thanking Chief De La Rosa for his leadership, the City for its support, and their families for their patience and understanding of the demands of the profession.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Vice Mayor Anderson read the proclamation declaring May 24, 2026 as Christ Journey Church Day in the City of Coral Gables, coinciding with the church's 100th anniversary celebration. The proclamation recognized the church, originally founded in 1926 as University Baptist Church under the patronage of city founder George Merrick, for 100 years of service to the South Florida community. It noted the congregation's multicultural, multi-generational character; its community outreach programs providing food, school support, and disaster relief; its global missions; and the forthcoming dedication of its new South Miami campus. A video was shown featuring Pastor White describing the church's founding, its survival after the hurricane of 1926, the eventual renaming from University Baptist Church to Christ Journey Church, and its continued mission of unity and outreach across hemispheres. Mayor Lago offered personal praise for Executive Pastor Ryan Reed, describing him as a consistent community presence. Church leadership in attendance included Chairman of the Board Luis Quevedo and Executive Pastor Ryan Reed. As a token of appreciation for all the City Commission does for the city, on behalf of Christ Journey Church a book written by Pastor Bill White entitled \ldblquote Upside Down Kingdom\rdblquote was distributed to everyone on the dais.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Parks and Recreation Director Fred Couceyro presented the proclamation for National Water Safety Month, noting that drowning remains one of the leading causes of death among children, making water safety a top priority at the Venetian Pool and the Country Club Pool. A representative from the Venetian Pool provided an update on summer operations, noting that lifeguard orientation had been completed and the pool was fully staffed. She also reported that the pool had recently received safety recognition from the Ellis Association, earning both Gold and Platinum awards for exceeding safety guidelines and standards. Director Couceyro highlighted the Venetian Aquatic Club, praising their work teaching children to swim and their active participation in the City's recent National Kids to Parks Day event. Mayor Lago commended the pool staff, noted a donation made by John Bell specifically to fund swim lessons for children, and congratulated Public Works and the contractor for the recently completed renovation of the Venetian Pool.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Police Chief Hudak presented the Officer of the Month award for March 2026 to Officer Julio Gonzalez of the midnight shift. Rather than a single defining incident, Officer Gonzalez was recognized for a sustained body of work throughout the month, during which he made nine arrests encompassing domestic violence, criminal mischief, methamphetamine possession, driving with a suspended or revoked license, illegal firearm possession, and individuals with outstanding warrants. Additionally, Police Chief Hudak noted that Officer Gonzalez simultaneously mentored a newer officer (a Probationary Police Officer), exemplifying the kind of leadership the department seeks as it integrates 37 new officers hired last year and an additional 11 currently in the academy. Officer Gonzalez accepted the award and thanked his supervisors, mentors (specifically Officer Castaneda and Lieutenant Lance), and his family.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Police Chief Hudak presented the Detective of the First Quarter Award for 2026 to Detective Miguel Becerra of the Economic Crimes Division. Detective Baserva was recognized for resolving two complex fraud and identity theft cases: Neiman Marcus Identity Theft Case: Detective Baserva identified and located a suspect responsible for fraudulent accounts involving a total loss of approximately $21,376. The suspect was located at Turner Guilford Knight Correctional Institute, where additional charges were filed. Contractor Fraud Case: A Coral Gables resident was defrauded of $15,000 after paying upfront to a fraudulent contractor. Detective Baserva tracked the suspect over several months, gave the individual the opportunity to come forward voluntarily, and upon refusal, used investigative skills to locate and arrest the individual, resulting in partial restitution for the resident. Chief Hudak emphasized the labor-intensive nature of economic crimes and fraud investigations, which involve extensive documentation, interagency coordination, and meticulous use of investigative tools. Detective Baserva accepted the award with humility, crediting his entire unit and expressing particular concern for elderly residents, who he noted are disproportionately victimized in fraud cases.\cf1\f1\par
}

