{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago introduced Agenda Item A-1 and invited Olga Ramudu, a Coral Gables resident, active civic participant, and friend to say a few words. He introduced her with warm remarks and spoke of her tireless engagement on behalf of the city in diplomatic and business forums, often appearing alongside Director Belkys Perez-Economic Development and her team to welcome international dignitaries and ambassadors. Mayor Lago read the Resolution of Condolence into the record on behalf of the Commission, honoring the life of Daniel Ramudu, beloved son of Olga Ramudu and Coral Gables resident, who passed away recently. The resolution recognized Daniel's distinguished professional career from his years as a trading associate in New York to founding his own real estate technology venture and celebrated his personal qualities: an adventurous and curious spirit, a love of discovering new restaurants and exploring new places, and above all, a deep and cherished bond with his daughter and his mother, with whom he worked closely in his later years. The resolution concluded with the Commission extending their deepest condolences to the Ramudu family and joining together in celebrating a life well lived, filled with hard work, love, curiosity, and connection. Olga Ramudu accepted the resolution with gratitude and said that her son had lived, worked, and played in Coral Gables just as she has, and that she knew he was watching from above and was grateful for the recognition. Mayor Lago concluded that Daniel was smart, kind, and resilient even in difficult moments, a reflection, he said, of his mother's character and strength.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Economic Development Director Belkys Perez presented Agenda Item A-2, announcing that July 2026 marks the fourth consecutive year of the Taste of the Gables program - a signature summer initiative designed to drive foot traffic and revenue to Coral Gables restaurants during the traditionally slow summer months. Since its launch, the program has grown from 30 participating restaurants to 75 in 2026. A survey of prior-year participants found that 70% reported revenue increases of 5 to 20% during the program period, with some restaurants experiencing even greater gains. Director Perez highlighted that the Economic Development Department secured more than $24,000 in combined cash and in-kind sponsorships this year, enabling broader and higher-quality promotion of the city's dining scene. She also introduced Gloria Baez, the new Vice President of the Coral Gables Chamber of Commerce, and welcomed her collaboration as a new partner. Two participating restaurant representatives were also recognized:\par
\bullet Let Them Have Cake - Owner Estefania (architect-turned-artisan cake designer), located at 367 Alhambra Circle. The Mayor enthusiastically praised the quality of her custom cakes and coffee and noted that he attended the ribbon cutting approximately two years prior. Commissioner Lara also confirmed he and his wife are regular patrons.\par
\bullet Aromas del Peru - A Coral Gables fixture for 16 years, recently relocated from Ponce de Leon Boulevard to Giralda Avenue, where increased foot traffic generated by the City's streetscaping improvements has proven highly beneficial to the business. The restaurant representative described Taste of the Gables as a more locally focused and community-driven initiative compared to Miami Spice.\par
Vice Mayor Anderson read the proclamation into the record, formally declaring July 2026 as Taste of the Gables Restaurant Month. The proclamation recognized the program's role in supporting local economic health, highlighted the participation of Michelin-starred and locally beloved establishments, and noted that there is no cost for restaurants to participate, ensuring broad access for all dining establishments in the city. Director Perez concluded by publicly thanking her staff members Solange Lopez and Danielle Cordova for manually inputting all restaurant photos and menus into the program platform.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Fire Chief De La Rosa introduced Agenda Item A-3 He introduced the Firefighter of the Month recognitions for July 2026 to Lieutenant Milito from the City's newly formed Emergency Medical Services (EMS) Division and Assistant Fire Chief Jones presented the awards, framing the recognition around the theme "you don't go as far as your dream, you go as far as your team." Lieutenant Carlo Milito was recognized for his exceptional preparation and administrative leadership within the EMS Division. Lieutenant Milito and Captain Muslera together ensured that a State of Florida site inspection of the EMS Division resulted in zero deficiencies, while also identifying areas of process improvement and operational efficiency. Lt. Milito expressed gratitude to his wife, daughter, and the Fire Department leadership for enabling him to pursue his work both in the field and in administration.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Fire Chief de la Rosa introduced Captain Muslera and Assistant Fire Chief Jones made the presentation. Captain Christina Muslera serves as the EMS Captain for Coral Gables Fire, she was recognized alongside Lieutenant Milito for the flawless State inspection outcome and their collaborative contributions to the newly formed EMS Division. She expressed gratitude to Fire Chief De La Rosa, Assistant Fire Chiefs Jones, Legra, Fletcher and Lieutenant Milito for their mentorship and support over the prior seven months. Fire Chief De La Rosa also took the opportunity to welcome back Chief Fajardo, who had recently returned from a Task Force 2 deployment.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Officer Challenger was recognized for an incident which occurred on April 14, 2026, involving a burglary in progress at 5526 Majorie Street. The homeowner, who was not home at the time, had received a Ring camera alert showing the subject in a white hoodie with a black beard and two brown backpacks attempting to enter through the back door. The subject fled before officers arrived. Using his observation and investigative skills, Officer Challenger canvassed the area, located a subject on the 400 block of Como Avenue matching the description, and arranged for the victim to be brought to the scene for a positive identification. The subject, who claimed he was simply looking for food, waived his Miranda rights and was taken into custody. Officer Challenger accepted the award with humility, crediting the outcome to teamwork. He acknowledged Officer Castaneda, who had previously identified the subject during a field interview the day before and who immediately shared a photo when the call came in, as well as Officers Molina and Luis Lopez, who were on scene with him. He also noted that Lieutenant Baena almost beat him to the apprehension of the subject. Commissioner Castro presented Officer Challenger with two commemorative coins: the Coral Gables centennial coin and the America 250 coin and expressed words of appreciation. Officer Challenger also acknowledged Aaron Glasser, who works behind the scenes to celebrate Officer of the Month and Officer of the Year honorees and whose contributions to the police department and the FOP often go unrecognized.\par
\pard\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Police Deputy Chief Hanlon made the presentation recognizing Officer Jorge Brenes as Officer of the Month for May 2026. He stated that between April 21st and April 30th, approximately 20 property crime incidents were reported in a residential area adjacent to the golf course, with a suspect identified as going through unlocked vehicles. Detectives compiled cases and identified the suspect in a photograph, issuing a probable cause flyer to officers. Despite active drone surveillance and coordinated searches, the suspect remained at large. On May 6th, while still on a field training assignment with his Field Training Officer (FTO), Officer Brenes was in the 7-Eleven store at 350 Bird Road when he immediately recognized the suspect from the departmental flyer. The subject was detained, searched and was found in possession of multiple stolen credit cards and IDs. Detectives were called to the scene, and the subject was placed under arrest. The apprehension ultimately allowed investigators to close approximately 14 cases across multiple jurisdictions, including cases in Miami Beach and the City of Miami. Officer Brenes, who is early in his law enforcement career, accepted the honor with humility, specifically crediting his FTOs - Detective Rivera, Officers Becker, Azate, and Luis Lopez - for their patience, high standards, and mentorship. He acknowledged that Officers Luis Lopez and Alex Rodriguez were with him at the time of the apprehension and that the detention was executed as a smooth team effort with no incident. Mayor Lago commended Officers Challenger and Brenes for their work in maintaining Coral Gables as one of the safest communities in South Florida.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Asst. Director Human Resources & Risk Management Jose Rodriguez presented Item A-7, recognizing Javier Romero, a part-time producer and editor in the City Manager/Communications Department, as the Part-Time Employee of the Quarter for the second quarter of 2026. Romero joined the city in the fall of last year and was nominated by Communications Director Martha Pantin. Director Pantin praised Romero for his outstanding personal initiative, citing three specific examples: (1) he independently identified an aesthetic issue with the Commission Chambers backdrop and developed and implemented a solution - a curtain installation - within 24 hours before a formal fix was arranged; he conceived and launched a recurring social media series called "Did You Know," a fun and educational format for Instagram and other platforms that highlights City programs and departments in an accessible and engaging way for residents; and he independently arranged to have cables that had been running along the conference room floor properly concealed, improving the space for visitors meetings and delegations. Romero accepted the award graciously, acknowledging Director Pantin's leadership and nomination, the mentorship of colleague Michael Rocha, and the support of other colleagues, friends, family, and girlfriend. He noted it was unusual but meaningful to be in front of the cameras rather than behind them.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red11\green11\blue11;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Jose Rodriguez Assistant Director-Human Resources & Risk Director presented Item A-8, recognizing Robert Chang, a Parking Enforcement Specialist with nearly 20 years of service to the city, as the Employee of the Month for April 2026. Monica Beltran-Director of Parking, Sustainability and Mobility Services described this recognition as a uniquely compelling case of going above and beyond the scope of the job and went into detail stating that \cf1 around 9:30 a.m., Officer Chang was driving a city vehicle to a car wash when he heard a school bus honking repeatedly. While other drivers passed by, he stopped and found a child trapped inside - the driver had dropped off the other passengers and left, unaware the child remained on board. The child, frightened and crying, had been trying to get help for about two hours before Officer - Chang pried open the doors. He immediately called the child's parent (checking for food allergies before offering food), his supervisor, and law enforcement, and stayed with the child until the distraught mother arrived.\cf0 \cf1 Director Beltran noted the heat could have made the outcome fatal and pointed to the incident as proof that parking officers are expected to act as community ambassadors, not just enforcers. Mayor Lago praised Officer Chang's vigilance and commitment to going beyond his defined role.\cf2\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Jose Rodriguez-Assistant Director & Human Resources Department recognized Jose Vilar, Aquatics Manager from the Community Recreation Department, as the \ldblquote Employee of the Month\rdblquote for May 2026. The recognition was described by Carolina Vester, Assistant City Manager as both long overdue and especially timely, given that Vilar had recently informed City leadership of his intention to pursue a career change and, in an extraordinary act of professional responsibility, had given the city a 10-month notice to allow adequate time to hire and train his replacement. Vilar's ties to the Venetian Pool began in childhood, when he learned to swim through the Venetian Aquatic Club. He later volunteered there as a swim instructor work he saw as vital, since swimming lessons are the top defense against drowning. That early involvement grew into a decade-plus career as Aquatics Manager, overseeing the safety of thousands of annual visitors at one of the City's busiest facilities. The nominating staff member praised his operational excellence, his culture of accountability and mentorship, and his focus on training and certifications as key drivers of innovation. He was commended for his proactive, forward-thinking leadership and pursuit of excellence. Vilar accepted the honor humbly, calling his 18 seasons with the city a blessing and thanking his team and colleagues. Mayor Lago wished him well and expressed hope he'd one day return.\cf1\f1\par
}

