{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Vice Mayor Anderson read the proclamation formally declaring September 2026 as Hunger Action Month in Coral Gables, recognizing Feeding South Florida's 45 years of service addressing hunger across Palm Beach, Broward, Miami-Dade, and Monroe Counties, and calling on all residents, businesses, and organizations to take meaningful action against food insecurity during the month. Cosmo Kumani, Director of Strategic Gifts for Feeding South Florida in Miami-Dade County, accepted the proclamation and outlined the organization's reach: 1.7 million individuals served annually across four counties, and over 553,000 Miami-Dade households reached through direct programs and partner agencies. Programs include community and mobile food pantries, school pantries, summer meals for children, home-delivered meals for seniors, SNAP benefits outreach, and workforce training in culinary arts, warehousing, and logistics. Vice Mayor Anderson concluded with a request that the city use its newsletter and communications platforms to promote donation opportunities and raise awareness of Hunger Action Month among residents.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Fire Chief De La Rosa recognized Firefighter Wilfredo "Willie" Gonzalez as Firefighter of the Month for August 2026, citing both his sustained contributions as a member of the department's Special Response Team (SRT) and a specific act of heroism that prompted his nomination. In late May, while dining at a restaurant in the Florida Keys on his day off and outside his jurisdiction, Gonzalez observed a patron at an adjacent table begin to choke. He immediately intervened, performed the Heimlich maneuver, and successfully cleared the obstruction - preventing what the Chief described as an otherwise certain tragedy. Gonzalez accepted the award humbly, thanking his supervisors and his family. Mayor Lago praised him for exemplifying the values of the Coral Gables Fire Department even while off-duty, noting that without his quick action the outcome could have been fatal.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Chief Hudak recognized Detective Natalie Rivera of the Youth Resource Unit nominated by Sergeant Escobar as Detective of the Second Quarter for 2026. The case that earned her the recognition began on May 31, 2026, when a father reported that his son, a student with cerebral palsy, was being extorted by two classmates who sent him violent videos, called him derogatory names, and threatened physical harm unless he paid them over $900. Detective Rivera responded swiftly, identified both subjects, established probable cause, located their residences, and apprehended both individuals within two weeks. Chief Hudak praised her for bringing both professional skill and genuine compassion to the investigation, expressing that her intervention improved the victim's daily life and provided a meaningful early intercession for the two accused. Detective Rivera accepted the award with sincere gratitude, expressing that she loves her work and that having it recognized means a great deal. Chief Hudak added a warm closing note, acknowledging that Detective Rivera's husband serves as the Sergeant of the Traffic Homicide Unit calling them one of the department's most productive couples and inviting him to join her at the podium.\cf1\f1\par
}

