}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Police Chief Edward Hudak presented the department's two newest mounted patrol horses, Merrick and Scarlet, joined by the officers who ride them, Officer Lisdey Hernandez (partnered with Merrick) and Officer Ashley Sheran (partnered with Scarlet). Chief Hudak traced the mounted unit's origin to a 2019 pilot program proposed by Officer Sheran, inspired in part by the department's history: Cyril Guest, the first Coral Gables police officer killed in the line of duty, was pictured on horseback in 1926, and the unit was framed as a revival of that legacy. The program began with a single rescue horse, Rockaway, who proved unexpectedly popular with residents (complete with birthday and retirement parties), before the department partnered with Miami-Dade County Parks to board and expand the unit, eventually training officers from other agencies, including the Miami-Dade Sheriff's Office. Officers Hernandez and Sheran described Merrick (a four-year-old Percheron, about 1,700 pounds) and Scarlet (a three-year-old Belgian cross) as central to community policing efforts - patrolling neighborhoods, appearing at events, and serving as an accessible, friendly point of contact between police and residents. Commissioner Lara praised the unit's community impact and moved to direct staff to study expanding the mounted patrol program; he also floated requiring Chief Hudak to wear the mounted unit's cowboy hat as part of his uniform), with Vice Mayor Anderson recalling attending Rockaway's first birthday party when she ran as a candidate in 2020 and Commissioner Fernandez noting the strong turnout from neighborhood children whenever Rockaway visited local parks.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Chief Hudak presented the department's newly acquired amphibious rescue vehicle, known as the Sherp, capable of operating on land and in water and seating up to six people. He explained that the vehicle grew out of the department's role responding to hurricanes and natural disasters elsewhere in Florida, a mission the commission had previously supported by resolution. Rather than requesting local tax dollars, the department worked with the city's state legislative delegation - Senator Ileana Garcia and area state representatives - to secure a state appropriation that funded the purchase at no cost to city taxpayers. Chief Hudak noted the vehicle would supplement the department's existing MRAP (a military-surplus armored vehicle) and would allow officers to reach residents in low-lying areas during flooding or other emergencies. Vice Mayor Anderson highlighted her own role in co-writing the funding request letter to Senator Garcia alongside Chief Hudak and Fire Chief De La Rosa, framing it as an example of effective local-to-state collaboration. \cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Vice Mayor Anderson read into the record a proclamation declaring September 17 through 23, 2026 as Constitution Week in Coral Gables, marking the 239th anniversary of the signing of the U.S. Constitution and the effort's origins in a 1955 petition by the Daughters of the American Revolution (DAR) asking Congress to formally designate the observance. The proclamation was presented to the Coral Gables Chapter of the DAR. A longtime chapter member, former Mayor Dorothy Thomson noted having belonged to the organization since 1969 and having previously served as chapter regent, spoke about the chapter's history in the city, including its early meetings at the Coral Gables Merrick House, and introduced the chapter's current regent, Penelope Johnson who accepted the proclamation on the chapter's behalf, tying the observance to the broader 250th anniversary of the Declaration of Independence and describing it as a reminder of the shared responsibility to preserve the country's founding ideals. \cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Fire Chief Marcos De La Rosa recognized Firefighter Daniel Martinez as the City's Firefighter of the Month for September 2026. Chief De La Rosa noted that Martinez, in only two years with the department, has already embraced additional training opportunities, including service on the department's Rapid Intervention Team, a specialized group that trains in rescuing firefighters in life-threatening situations and studies lessons learned from line-of-duty-death reports nationally. Chief De La Rosa also highlighted that Martinez organized and led the department's contingent of 22 firefighters in the memorial stair climb at the Hard Rock Stadium - climbing 110 stories in honor of first responders - held in connection with the 25th anniversary of September 11th. In accepting the recognition, Firefighter Martinez thanked Chief De La Rosa, the command staff, and specifically his crew at Station Two, including his captain and driver engineer, his partner on the truck, and his girlfriend for her support, and said he was proud to continue growing as a Coral Gables firefighter.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Chief Hudak recognized Officer Anibal Guerrero as Officer of the Month for July 2026. Chief Hudak recounted that on July 9, 2026, around 6:00 p.m., Officer Guerrero conducted what began as a routine traffic stop near Ponce de Leon Boulevard and Miracle Mile. During the stop, Guerrero determined the driver lacked a valid license and, continuing to investigate rather than simply issuing a citation, discovered the individual was using a false identity to conceal three active fugitive warrants out of Alachua County, including charges related to racketeering, conspiracy, and probation violations tied to prior cocaine trafficking and possession offenses dating back to 2012. The individual was taken into custody and returned to Alachua County to face those charges. In accepting the award, Officer Guerrero thanked the department's command staff, fellow officers, and specifically credited his colleagues who assisted with the arrest, including his sergeant and a detective, noting that the award reflected a team effort.\cf1\f1\par
}

