\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago welcomed representatives of Glory House, a faith-based organization serving survivors of human trafficking, and spoke of his longstanding involvement with the organization. A Glory House representative described the organization\rquote s history, which began in 2011, and its services to survivors, including a residential home for adult women that first opened in 2014, closed following the COVID-19 pandemic, and has since reopened at an undisclosed location, together with non-residential case management and referral services. She noted that human trafficking occurs throughout the community, including Coral Gables, and announced the organization\rquote s upcoming gala. Mayor Lago recalled the City\rquote s past efforts on the issue, including the prohibition of hourly motels and a joint enforcement task force with the City of Miami, and thanked the City\rquote s Police Department and the organization\rquote s supporters. The Mayor requested, with the Commission\rquote s consent, that the City Manager promote Glory House (gloryhouseofmiami.org) in the City\rquote s e-News to encourage resident involvement, donations, and volunteer service.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago presented a Resolution of Condolence to the family of Yvette Diaz, a member of the Finance Department who served the City for more than 22 years, most recently as Senior Payroll Specialist and previously as an Administrative Assistant. Members of her family attended, including her father, Sam Poliziano, and her sons. Following remarks by Mayor Lago, Finance Director Diana Gomez, Mr. Poliziano, a family member, Commissioner Fernandez, Commissioner Lara, and Vice Mayor Anderson, each of whom spoke of Ms. Diaz\rquote s professionalism, kindness, and deep faith, the Mayor asked for a moment of silence and read the resolution into the record. The Mayor requested, and the City Manager agreed, that a tribute to Ms. Diaz, including remembrances from the Finance Director and colleagues, be published in the City\rquote s e-News and on the City\rquote s website. The Commission indicated its concurrence.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1\qj\f0\fs24 Vice Mayor Anderson read into the record, and presented, a proclamation declaring September 2026 as Service Dog Awareness Month in Coral Gables. The proclamation was accepted by Terry O\rquote Connor, a puppy raiser with Canine Companions, who attended with a service-dog puppy in training. Ms. O\rquote Connor described the organization\rquote s work, which was founded in 1975, in placing expertly trained service dogs, at no cost, with persons with disabilities and veterans, and noted the continuing need for volunteer puppy raisers. Mayor Lago and Vice Mayor Anderson commended the organization and asked that information on volunteering and donating be provided to the City Manager, through the Vice Mayor, for distribution to the community.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago welcomed Superintendent Rafael Villalobos to the meeting. School Board Chair Marietta Rojas introduced the Superintendent, noting his more than three decades of service with Miami-Dade County Public Schools as a teacher, assistant principal, principal, and district administrator. Superintendent Villalobos thanked the Commission, spoke of the District\rquote s results, including its A rating for seven consecutive years and a graduation rate above 95 percent, and stated his commitment to continuing the partnership between the District and the City.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Chair Rojas noted that October is National Principals Month and asked the principals in attendance to stand for recognition. Mayor Lago read a certificate of recognition into the record, and the Clerk read the names of the principals, who were presented with certificates of recognition: Janet Sierra, Patricia Fairclough, Lucy Amengual, Ana Alvarez Arim\'f3n, Marisol Garrido, Dr. Michelle Sanchez-Perez, Ileana Artime, Angela Gonzalez, Dr. Alina D\'edaz-Blanco, and Anthony Balboa. Superintendent Villalobos and Chair Rojas thanked the City for the recognition, and Chair Rojas invited the community to the upcoming National Night Out event at Phillips Park on October 6. Mayor Lago thanked the principals for their service.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Taken with Item A-5. Superintendent Villalobos and Chair Rojas urged support for Referendum 268, the renewal of the referendum on the November ballot to supplement teacher and instructional-staff salaries and to support school police, and the Superintendent noted that a town hall on the referendum would be held the following evening, September 30, at Miami High School at 6:00 p.m. Mayor Lago, Vice Mayor Anderson, and Commissioners Fernandez and Lara each expressed support for the referendum and for public education. The Mayor recalled the City\rquote s early action in placing City police officers in the schools, and Chair Rojas thanked the City for its continued support of local schools.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Fire Chief De La Rosa introduced Fire Division Chief Louie Fajardo, a member of Task Force 2 (Urban Search and Rescue), who was deployed with the team to Venezuela following the twin earthquakes in June, noting that the team\rquote s rescue, performed jointly with a Chilean team, had been unofficially recognized by FEMA as the longest rescue and save from a structural collapse. Chief Fajardo presented a video and slide presentation describing the deployment of approximately 80 members, the logistical challenges of the mission, and the multi-day rescue of a trapped security guard. In response to a question from Commissioner Lara, he described the hazards the rescuers faced. He concluded by noting that the City\rquote s Technical Rescue Team has comparable capabilities, citing a recent local rope rescue of a driver from a vehicle in a parking garage.\cf1\f1\par
}

