\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago read into the record a Resolution of Condolence honoring Ellen Uguccioni, a pioneering preservationist whose vision and lifelong advocacy helped protect the architectural and historic character of Coral Gables. The resolution recognized that Ms. Uguccioni began her service with the City in 1983 as its Historic Preservation Officer, later became the first Director of the City's Historic Preservation Department, and, working closely with Mayor Dorothy Thompson, played an instrumental role in saving and restoring the historic Biltmore Hotel. The resolution further recognized her role as a founder of the Historic Preservation Association of Coral Gables, a past president of the Villagers, a lecturer at the University of Miami School of Architecture, an author, a twelve-year member of the Florida Historic Preservation Commission, and a recipient of the Florida Trust for Historic Preservation's Lifetime Achievement Award, as well as her 21 years of service in the United States Marine Corps, from which she retired with the rank of Lieutenant Colonel. Ms. Uguccioni passed away on September 27, 2026, at the age of 75. Tom Prescott of the Biltmore Hotel read into the record a letter from former Mayor Dorothy Thompson recalling Ms. Uguccioni's work overseeing the renovation of the Biltmore Hotel and shared his own recollections of her advocacy for the hotel's National Historic Landmark designation. A friend and former student of Ms. Uguccioni shared personal remarks, including memories of Ms. Uguccioni's final trolley tour of the City and her funeral with full military honors. Karelia Carbonell of the Historic Preservation Association of Coral Gables read a tribute on behalf of the organization, and the Historical Resources & Cultural Arts Director expressed the office's gratitude for Ms. Uguccioni's pioneering role in preservation in Coral Gables and South Florida. Vice Mayor Anderson shared remarks on Ms. Uguccioni's legacy, presented a video clip of Ms. Uguccioni recorded at the Biltmore Hotel during her 2023 trolley tour of the City, and stated that she would provide the tour itinerary to the City Clerk for the record. A photograph was taken with those in attendance.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Assistant Director for Mobility and Sustainability Matt Anderson presented the Keep Coral Gables Beautiful 2026 Commercial Beautification Award to Torre Companies, located at 208 Andalusia Avenue. Mr. Anderson explained that this was the fifth award cycle of the program, which began in 2022 as a partnership among Keep Coral Gables Beautiful, Economic Development, and Code Enforcement, with the Sustainability Advisory Board voting on the nominees. Torre Companies was one of four 2026 recipients and was recognized for going above and beyond in pressure cleaning, window cleaning, and tastefully decorating its storefront, including during the holidays. Venny Torre accepted the award on behalf of the company, expressing his family's commitment to keeping the downtown clean and to promoting culture and the arts. Mayor Lago commended Mr. Torre for the meticulous upkeep of his buildings, alley, and sidewalks, and requested, through the City Manager, that the outside consultant preparing the downtown improvement plan, together with Economic Development and the Chamber of Commerce, identify other exemplary properties to be highlighted as examples of the standard expected in the City. Vice Mayor Anderson noted that she had not received a single complaint regarding any of Torre Companies' development sites and commended the company's efforts to preserve healthy trees. Commissioner Fernandez commended the company for its pride in maintaining its properties and construction projects consistent with the City's history and original design intent. A photograph was taken.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago read into the record a Certificate of Recognition honoring architect Albert J. Socol, in observance of National Architecture Month. The certificate recognized that Mr. Socol earned his master's degree in architecture with honors from the University of Buenos Aires in 1965 and has devoted more than 35 years to designing and building major projects across the State of Florida, including the School of Architecture at the University of Florida, the James L. Knight Convention Center and Hyatt Hotel in Miami, and the Courthouse Center in downtown Miami. In Coral Gables, Mr. Socol designed 150 Alhambra Circle, 2199 Ponce de Leon Boulevard, and 2100 Ponce de Leon Boulevard, which earned several City Beautiful awards and are featured in the 2026 book Coral Gables, The Moderns, as well as the French Village on Segovia. Mr. Socol thanked the Commission and reflected on his career, including the start of his practice at Douglas Entrance, his decision to become both architect and developer, and the guidance he received from the City in building successful projects that respected the City's standards. He also recognized his wife for her role in preserving the Chinese Village and in the publication of the book, the proceeds of which benefit historic preservation. It was announced that a copy of the book would be donated to the City's Historic Resources Archive. A photograph was taken.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Assistant Human Resources Director Jose Rodriguez introduced the proclamation recognizing National Disability Employment Awareness Month and, together with Resolution H-1, the Principles of Inclusion. Mr. Rodriguez explained that, rather than recognizing a business or individual as in prior years, the City was recognizing the Advisory Board on Disability Affairs itself for its advocacy, including its support of Adventure Day, Project Victory, the annual autism acceptance flag-raising ceremony, and a recent training for the City's leadership team on inclusion in the workplace. Board Chair Justine Chichester accepted the recognition on behalf of the Board, thanked the City for its commitment to accessibility and inclusion, and announced an employment pathway collaboration event to be held on October 23, 2026, connecting businesses with organizations to create employment opportunities for neurodivergent young adults. Additional remarks were offered by members of the Board and by Jen Sando, who spoke about the autism programs now available at David Fairchild Elementary and Ponce de Leon Middle School as a result of the City's collaboration with the School Board. Mayor Lago thanked the Board for its leadership, spoke about his own experience with dyslexia, and asked that the October 23rd event be promoted through the City's newsletter. Commissioner Fernandez thanked the Board members for the time they commit to the City. Vice Mayor Anderson shared personal reflections on disability and read the proclamation proclaiming October 2026 as Disability Acceptance Month in the City of Coral Gables.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Assistant Fire Chief of Operations Xavier Jones presented the Firefighter of the Month award to Lieutenant Eldad Simovitch, noting that the Lieutenant was one of his own classmates and a longtime friend. Assistant Chief Jones described how, approximately three weeks earlier, Lieutenant Simovitch came upon a motorcycle accident while en route to duty, stopped, blocked traffic, and rendered aid to the critically injured motorcyclist, who was subsequently transported to Ryder Trauma Center. He further noted that two years earlier Lieutenant Simovitch had spent seven days of his personal time volunteering with fire rescue in Israel, working in their stations and responding to calls. Lieutenant Simovitch thanked the Commission and stated that any Coral Gables firefighter would have done the same, whether on or off duty. Mayor Lago commended Lieutenant Simovitch as a man of few words but of action and thanked him for his service to the residents and business community. City Manager Iglesias added that the Fire Chief was at the State Emergency Operations Center assisting with hurricane recovery efforts in the northwest part of the State. A photograph was taken.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\qj\f0\fs24 Police Chief Hudak recognized Officers Reynaldo Cortez and Ariel Garcia as Officers of the Month for August 2026 for their actions during a single incident. Chief Hudak explained that in the early morning hours of August 1, 2026, while conducting a saturation patrol, the officers observed two individuals in the 100 block of Antilla Avenue near Crystal Academy. When the officers approached, one individual fled and hid on the school grounds, then ran through the perimeter established by responding officers before being taken into custody. A canvass of the area located vehicle lug nuts where the subject had been hiding and a nearby vehicle resting on concrete blocks with its tires removed. Chief Hudak credited the officers' diligent patrol techniques and keen sense of what was out of place. Both officers thanked the Chief, the Deputy Chief, the command staff, and the awards selection committee, and credited their teamwork. Mayor Lago thanked the officers for their hard work, reaffirmed the Commission's commitment to law and order and to providing the Police Department with the resources it needs, and noted the record reductions in crime reported by the Chief. A photograph was taken.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\qj\f0\fs24 Assistant Human Resources Director Jose Rodriguez introduced the Employee of the Month presentations, noting that the employees being recognized go above and beyond on a daily basis and exemplify what it means to be a public servant. Mr. Rodriguez recognized Noel Polo of the Public Works Department as Employee of the Month. Mr. Polo has served the City as a construction manager for more than 33 years and is the City's second most tenured full-time employee, behind only Police Chief Hudak, and is known for his professionalism, dedication, positive attitude, and institutional knowledge, as well as for mentoring countless employees. Public Works Director Hermes Diaz shared remarks commending Mr. Polo as a tireless employee who is usually the first to respond, day or night, to maintain the City's sewer and stormwater utility, and who embodies the Department's motto of "keep it flowing." Mr. Polo thanked the Commission and remarked on the pride he takes when residents learn that Public Works does "everything" for the City.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Human Resources and Risk Management Director Clifford Friedman introduced the item, and Assistant Human Resources Director Jose Rodriguez recognized Cesar Hechavarria as Employee of the Month. Mr. Rodriguez explained that Mr. Hechavarria joined the City in 2019 as a Personnel Specialist responsible for recruitment and was promoted in 2022 to Recruitment Manager, where he leads a team of four recruiters responsible for ensuring that all City departments maintain the staffing levels necessary to serve the community. Under his leadership, the recruitment team has streamlined the hiring process, improved time to hire, and maintained record-low vacancy numbers, and has filled some of the City's most difficult and specialized positions, with Mr. Hechavarria and his team approaching nearly 1,000 hires, including 100 to 120 summer camp employees each year. Mr. Rodriguez also noted Mr. Hechavarria's role as an ambassador for the city at career fairs and networking events and as a skilled negotiator of salaries and benefits. Mr. Hechavarria thanked Mr. Rodriguez for the introduction, expressed his gratitude for the opportunity to serve in his role, and acknowledged his Human Resources team.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qj\f0\fs24 Assistant Human Resources Director Jose Rodriguez recognized Odalys Ferreiro of the Finance Department as Employee of the Month for September. Ms. Ferreiro joined the City in 2023 as a Senior Administrative Analyst and was nominated by Finance Director Diana Gomez for her dedication, initiative, and coordination skills. Ms. Gomez shared remarks noting that Ms. Ferreiro has become an integral part of the Finance Department and that she took the lead in coordinating the department's recent office move and setup while the Director was out of town, keeping everything organized and making the transition as smooth as possible for the entire department. Ms. Gomez added that Ms. Ferreiro is always willing to help and provides support to the entire department. Ms. Ferreiro thanked the Commission, expressed her appreciation to the Finance Director for her guidance over the past several years, and thanked her colleagues across the Finance Department's divisions.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\qj\f0\fs24 Community Recreation Director Fred Couceyro announced that the Community Recreation Department had been re-accredited by the National Recreation and Park Association. Mr. Couceyro explained that national accreditation confirms that the Department meets national standards for best practices in maintenance, safety, operations, planning, and programming, and requires an arduous process of providing evidence of a practiced methodology over a five-year period. Of the 156 standards, agencies must meet 96 percent to be accredited, and the Department met 100 percent. He credited the Department's staff and every City department that collaborated in providing documentation and noted that only 219 of the more than 10,000 parks and recreation agencies in the United States are nationally accredited, which is why the award is presented at a ceremony called "Best of the Best." Mayor Lago congratulated the Director and his team for the work they do at every park opening and City event, noted that the City's parks are the envy of Miami-Dade County, and expressed pride in the new park additions forthcoming in the downtown. A photograph was taken.\cf1\f1\par
}

{\rtf1\fbidis\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\froman\fprq2\fcharset0 Times New Roman;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\ltrpar\qj\f0\fs24 Mayor Lago announced that Items A-11 and H-2 would be heard together. The item opened with a performance by Lovell Rose of Actors' Playhouse of a selection from the musical Jekyll & Hyde. The Historical Resources & Cultural Arts Director noted that this year's theme for National Arts and Humanities Month is "stories that unite us." Cultural Development Board Chair Jose Vald\'e9s-Fauli congratulated the City on its commitment to the arts, noting that the City has invested $6.8 million in cultural affairs since 2005; that 46 grants were approved out of 53 applications this grant period, a 30 percent increase in applicants; that more than 1,300 cultural events are offered free throughout the year at 30 venues; and that the organizations and their audiences generate an estimated $20 million in expenditures. Mayor Lago thanked Chair Vald\'e9s-Fauli and the Board for their service. Artistic Director David Arisco and Executive Director Barbara Stein of Actors' Playhouse shared remarks on behalf of the City's cultural grantees, with Ms. Stein noting the theater's inclusive programming and the late Ellen Uguccioni's role in the renovation of the Miracle Theatre. Commissioner Castro spoke about the Miracle Theatre's special place in the community, and Commissioner Lara read the proclamation proclaiming October 2026 as National Arts and Humanities Month in Coral Gables and recognizing the City's 2026-2027 cultural grant recipients and partner organizations. Vice Mayor Anderson noted that the City is one of the few communities in Florida with a cultural grant program and commended the grantees for offering opportunities to those who cannot afford tickets. Under Item H-2, the Historical Resources & Cultural Arts Director presented six new permanent public artworks from municipal and private development projects, including works at 427 Biltmore Way, the Aragon Plaza, Regency Park, and the Museum Plaza, together with an update on the Intervals temporary public art projects at five locations along Miracle Mile, to be celebrated during Art Week with a VIP reception at 427 Biltmore Way. Mayor Lago commended the selection of artists and requested, through the City Manager, that the artist's foundation be invited to the event, that the alley at the installation site be placed in impeccable condition, and that staff determine whether the City or adjacent property owners are responsible for maintaining alleys throughout the City. \cf1\f1\par
}

